@* Token-size comparison. Uses MEASURED classical sizes and the ACTUAL measured
   PQ token size from this build, on a shared scale, so the gap is honest rather
   than illustrative. *@
<div class="sizebar">
    <h3>Token size vs classical</h3>
    @{
        // Measured against the standard iss/aud/sub/exp claim set the benchmark
        // suite uses (benchmarks/PostQuantum.Jwt.Benchmarks/TokenSizeReport.cs).
        // ES256 is the directly measured value from JsonWebTokenHandler on .NET
        // 10; HS256 and EdDSA share that header + payload and only differ in
        // signature length (HMAC-SHA256: 32 B raw → 43 base64url chars; Ed25519
        // and ECDSA P-256: 64 B raw → 86 base64url chars), so they are computed
        // by signature-delta from the ES256 anchor. A user's token will differ
        // by tens of bytes from these if they type longer/shorter claim values
        // — the comparison is against a representative real-world claim set,
        // not against the exact bytes the playground just produced.
        const int hs256 = 272;   // HMAC-SHA256  (ES256 - (86 - 43) = 272)
        const int es256 = 315;   // ECDSA P-256  (measured, JsonWebTokenHandler)
        const int eddsa = 315;   // Ed25519      (same signature length as ES256)
        int max = Math.Max(PqBytes, 1);
    }
    <div class="bar"><span class="lbl">HS256</span><span class="track"><span class="fill c1" style="width:@Pct(hs256, max)%"></span></span><span class="val">~@hs256 B</span></div>
    <div class="bar"><span class="lbl">ES256</span><span class="track"><span class="fill c1" style="width:@Pct(es256, max)%"></span></span><span class="val">~@es256 B</span></div>
    <div class="bar"><span class="lbl">EdDSA</span><span class="track"><span class="fill c2" style="width:@Pct(eddsa, max)%"></span></span><span class="val">~@eddsa B</span></div>
    <div class="bar"><span class="lbl">@(Encrypted ? "PQ enc" : "PQ sig")</span><span class="track"><span class="fill c3" style="width:100%"></span></span><span class="val">@PqBytes B</span></div>
    <p class="hint small">
        Measured this build: <strong>@PqBytes bytes</strong>
        (@(Multiple(PqBytes, es256))&times; an ES256 token).
        ML-DSA-65 signatures are ~3.3&nbsp;KB; encryption roughly doubles the token
        (the whole signed JWT becomes the AES-GCM plaintext and is re-encoded, plus
        ~1.5&nbsp;KB of X-Wing KEM material).
        Comfortable in an <code>Authorization</code> header; usually too large for cookies.
    </p>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public int PqBytes { get; set; }
    [Parameter] public bool Encrypted { get; set; }
    private static int Pct(int v, int max) => Math.Clamp((int)Math.Round(100.0 * v / max), 1, 100);
    private static string Multiple(int v, int baseline) => baseline == 0 ? "—" : (v / (double)baseline).ToString("F0");
}
